'''alloquialism''' (a-lō′kwi-ạl-izm), ''n.'' [< ''alloquial'' + ''-ism.''] A phrase or manner of speech used in addressing. ''N. E. D.''

'''alloquy†''' (al′ọ̄-kwi), ''n.'' [< L. ''allóquium, adloquium'', < ''alloqui, adloqui'', speak to, address: see ''allocution.'' Cf. ''colloquy, soliloquy'', and ''obloquy.''] The act of speaking to another or others; an address.

'''Allorhina''' (al-ọ̄-rī′nä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄλλος, other, + ῥίς, ῥίν, nose.] A genus of lamellicorn beetles (''Scarabæidæ''), belonging to the tribe ''Cetoniini'', readily distinguished by the fact that the epimera of the mesothorax are visible from above as a triangular piece between the prothorax and the elytra, a character of rare occurrence in ''Coleoptera.'' {{smaller|The scutellum is covered by a prolongation of the base of the prothorax. The best-known species is ''A. nitida'' (Linnæus), very common in the more southern United States. It is a green velvety insect, nearly an inch long, of nearly square form, somewhat pointed in front, with the sides of the thorax}}

